'''''Lavergnesaurus''''' is an [[Extinction|extinct]] [[Monotypic taxon|monotypic genus]] of [[Skink|scincid]] [[lizard]] that lived in [[France]] during the [[Bartonian]] [[Stage (stratigraphy)|stage]] of the Eocene [[Geologic timescale|epoch]].

== Etymology ==
The [[Generic name (biology)|generic name]] ''Lavergnesaurus'' is derived from the locality of Lavergne in France and from the Greek word ''saura'', meaning lizard. The [[Binomial nomenclature|specific epithet]] of the [[type species]], ''lamarcki'', references [[Jean-Baptiste Lamarck]], an early [[Evolutionary biology|evolutionary biologist]] who developed the [[Lamarckism|theory of evolution by acquired characteristics]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Čerňanský |first1=Andrej |last2=Georgalis |first2=Georgios L. |last3=Orliac |first3=Maeva J. |last4=Martin |first4=Jeremy E. |date=2 April 2026 |title=The reptiles from the Lavergne locality of Phosphorites du Quercy in France show a high taxonomic diversity at the end of the middle Eocene (MP 16) |url=https://sjp.pensoft.net/article/178570/ |journal=[[Swiss Journal of Palaeontology]] |volume=145 |pages=343–401 |doi=10.3897/sjp.145.178570 |doi-access=free |issn=1664-2384 |access-date=2 April 2026}}</ref>

